> object_path can throw an exception if the path isn't valid in the passed in string.

How so? There is no check, the string is directly assigned.

```cpp
  struct string_path_wrapper
  {
      std::string str;

      string_path_wrapper() = default;
      string_path_wrapper(const string_path_wrapper&) = default;
      string_path_wrapper& operator=(const string_path_wrapper&) = default;
      string_path_wrapper(string_path_wrapper&&) = default;
      string_path_wrapper& operator=(string_path_wrapper&&) = default;
      ~string_path_wrapper() = default;

      string_path_wrapper(const std::string& str_in) : str(str_in) {}
      string_path_wrapper(std::string&& str_in) : str(std::move(str_in)) {}
```

I am looking at `string_path_wrapper(const std::string& str_in) : str(str_in) {}` here.

> yeah, you are correct, but the implicit conversion could throw, right?

I'm not super familiar with implicit conversions but if it calls the `object_path` constructor, that just assigns the string. `object_path` does not contribute anything new in terms of exceptions.

> > object_path can throw an exception if the path isn't valid in the passed in string.
> 
> How so? There is no check, the string is directly assigned.

Fair point.  For some reason I thought there were more checks on construction.  I think I got mixed up on your desire for an "always valid" object_path class.  If we agree we're not chasing that anymore, this patch seems fine.

